#include <charconv>
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>
#include <utility>

constexpr long long kMaxSpread = 10'000;

template <typename... Ts>
bool all_positive(Ts... xs) {
    return (... && (xs > 0));
}

std::optional<long long> parse_px(std::string_view text) {
    long long px = 0;
    const auto [end, ec] = std::from_chars(text.data(), text.data() + text.size(), px);
    if (ec != std::errc{} || end != text.data() + text.size()) return std::nullopt;
    return px;
}

int main() {
    auto spread = [](auto bid, auto ask) { return ask - bid; };
    std::cout << "max spread: " << kMaxSpread << '\n';
    std::cout << "all positive: " << all_positive(3, 5, 9) << ' ' << all_positive(3, -1) << '\n';

    const std::map<std::string, std::pair<long long, long long>> book{
        {"AAPL", {19270, 19280}}, {"MSFT", {41005, 41010}}};
    for (const auto& [symbol, quote] : book) {
        std::cout << symbol << ' ' << quote.first << '/' << quote.second << " spread "
                  << spread(quote.first, quote.second) << '\n';
    }
    for (std::string_view token : {"19275", "19x75"}) {
        if (const auto px = parse_px(token); px) {
            std::cout << "px " << *px << '\n';
        } else {
            std::cout << "px bad\n";
        }
    }
    std::cout << "spread as double: " << spread(101.25, 101.5) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
*What C++20 and C++23 Add* ran most of **C++20** in the sandbox: concepts, ranges and views, `std::span`, `<=>`, designated initializers, `consteval` and `constinit`, and coroutines, with modules and `std::format` from this Mac. C++20 also requires two's complement, as *Numbers in Memory: Binary, Two's Complement, and IEEE 754* said, and adds `std::jthread`, `[[likely]]`, and `<bit>`, whose `std::popcount` *Bitwise Operations* named beside `__builtin_popcount`.

**C++23**, finished in 2023 and published in 2024, is the newest published standard. Most of it is library: `std::print` and `std::expected`, which that lesson ran on this Mac, `std::mdspan`, `std::flat_map`, `std::generator`, and `std::ranges::to`. The language gets the `uz` suffix for a `std::size_t` literal, `if consteval`, which asks whether the code is running while compiling, an `m[i, j]` that takes two indexes, and **deducing this**, a member function whose first parameter names its own object, `this Self&& self`, so one function serves `const` and non-`const` objects. From this Mac, `clang++ -std=c++23`:

```cpp norun
#include <cmath>
#include <mdspan>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

constexpr double root(double x) {
    if consteval {  // while compiling: Newton's method, since std::sqrt isn't constexpr
        double r = x;
        for (int i = 0; i < 30; ++i) r = 0.5 * (r + x / r);
        return r;
    } else {
        return std::sqrt(x);  // at run time: the library's fast call
    }
}

int main() {
    for (auto i{0uz}; i < 3; ++i) std::print("{} ", i);  // 0uz is a std::size_t
    std::println();
    std::vector<double> px = {101.25, 101.27, 99.50, 99.52};
    std::mdspan quotes(px.data(), 2, 2);  // 2 symbols by bid and ask, over the same 4 doubles
    std::println("quotes[1, 0] = {:.2f}", quotes[1, 0]);
    constexpr double at_compile_time = root(2.0);
    std::println("{:.6f} {:.6f}", at_compile_time, root(static_cast<double>(px.size() / 2)));
    return 0;
}
```

```text
0 1 2 
quotes[1, 0] = 99.50
1.414214 1.414214
```

The gaps are real too. libc++ 21 has no `std::generator`, and C++23's `import std;`, one line for the whole standard library, stops at `unknown type name 'import'`, since Apple clang reads `import` only with `-fcxx-modules`, and with that flag at `fatal error: module 'std' not found`: the module has to be built from a source file this Mac's toolchain doesn't ship, and `clang++ -print-library-module-manifest-path` says `<NOT PRESENT>`. g++ 10.2 has none of C++23. Press Run:

```cpp error
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    for (auto i{0uz}; i < 3; ++i) std::cout << i << ' ';  // C++23
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

**C++26** is the next standard. The committee froze its features in June 2025 and finished its technical work in March 2026. The approval ballot then sent comments back, most of them about contracts, and the committee finished it again in November 2026, keeping contracts. As of late 2026, ISO hasn't published it, so treat its details as a draft until then:

- **reflection**, voted in June 2025: `^^Tick` is a value describing the type `Tick`, and `<meta>` asks it for names and members while compiling.
- **contracts**, voted in February 2025: `pre(qty > 0)` and `post(...)` on a function's declaration, and `contract_assert` in its body.
- new library: `std::execution` for asynchronous work, `std::inplace_vector`, a vector with a fixed capacity and no heap, `std::hive`, `std::simd`, and `std::linalg`.
- small pieces: `#embed` pastes a file's bytes in, `xs...[0]` indexes a pack, `= delete("reason")` explains itself, `_` names what you never use, and `std::add_sat` saturates instead of wrapping.

`clang++ -std=c++2c`, the working name, which `-std=c++26` also accepts, runs the small ones, with a 15-byte file `logon.txt` holding `8=FIX.4.2|35=A|` beside the source:

```cpp norun
#include <cstdint>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <utility>

template <typename... Ts>
auto first(Ts... xs) {
    return xs...[0];  // pack indexing
}

void send(std::int64_t ticks) { std::println("sent {}", ticks); }
void send(double) = delete("prices travel as integer ticks");  // = delete with a reason

constexpr unsigned char kLogon[] = {
#embed "logon.txt"  // the file's bytes, pasted in as a list of numbers
};

int main() {
    std::println("first: {}", first(19275, 2.5, 'x'));
    send(std::int64_t{19275});
    auto [_, qty] = std::pair{"AAPL", 300};  // _ is a name you never use
    auto [_, px] = std::pair{"MSFT", 41010};  // so it can repeat
    std::println("{} {}", qty, px);
    std::println("saturated: {}", std::add_sat<std::int8_t>(100, 100));  // 127, no wrap
    std::println("logon: {} bytes, starts with {:c}", sizeof kLogon, kLogon[0]);
    return 0;
}
```

```text
first: 19275
sent 19275
300 41010
saturated: 127
logon: 15 bytes, starts with 8
```

clang still warns that `#embed` is "a Clang extension", since it came from C23 first, and `send(192.75)` stops with `call to deleted function 'send': prices travel as integer ticks`. The big three haven't arrived: `#include <inplace_vector>` is `file not found`, `pre(x >= 0)` gets `expected function body after function declarator`, and `^^Tick` reads as Apple's own `^` blocks extension, `block pointer to non-function type is invalid`, the clash that led the committee to spell reflection `^^` and not `^`.

**Feature-test macros** say what you have, one feature at a time, which a single `__cplusplus` can't. The compiler predefines the language ones, like `__cpp_structured_bindings`, and each library header defines its own, like `__cpp_lib_optional`, all gathered in `<version>`, a C++20 header that g++ 10.2 has in every mode. A macro's value is the year and month of the version of the feature you have, so `__cpp_lib_optional` is `201606` in the sandbox and `202110` under this Mac's C++23, whose `optional` has C++23's `and_then`. `__has_include(<header>)`, from *The Preprocessor: Macros and Conditional Compilation*, adds whether a header exists at all. To look a feature up, cppreference marks its pages `(since C++17)`, `(deprecated in C++11)`, or `(removed in C++17)` and names each feature's macro, and its **C++ compiler support** page lists every feature by paper with the first g++, clang, MSVC, and Apple clang that has it. That page is what a team checks, for every compiler it builds with, before it moves to a newer standard.

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <new>
#include <version>

// C++17 names it, but g++ only from version 12, so fall back to a constant of our own
#if defined(__cpp_lib_hardware_interference_size)
constexpr std::size_t kLine = std::hardware_destructive_interference_size;
const char* kLineFrom = "the library";
#else
constexpr std::size_t kLine = 64;
const char* kLineFrom = "our own";
#endif

int main() {
#ifdef __cpp_structured_bindings
    std::cout << "__cpp_structured_bindings: " << __cpp_structured_bindings << '\n';
#endif
#ifdef __cpp_lib_optional
    std::cout << "__cpp_lib_optional: " << __cpp_lib_optional << '\n';
#endif
#ifdef __cpp_lib_filesystem
    std::cout << "__cpp_lib_filesystem: " << __cpp_lib_filesystem << '\n';
#endif
#ifdef __cpp_concepts
    std::cout << "__cpp_concepts: " << __cpp_concepts << '\n';
#else
    std::cout << "__cpp_concepts: missing\n";
#endif
#if __has_include(<format>)
    std::cout << "<format>: found\n";
#else
    std::cout << "<format>: missing\n";
#endif
    std::cout << "cache line constant: " << kLine << ", from " << kLineFrom << '\n';
    return 0;
}

